Step 4: Configure Nginx Virtual Host (server block)
Create a custom site configuration file:

sudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/static-site

Paste the following site block into nano:
server {
    listen 80;
    listen [::]:80;

    server_name _;
    root /var/www/static-site/public;
    index index.html;

    # Performance & Hardening
    add_header X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN" always;
    add_header X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff" always;

    location / {
        try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
    }

    # Static Asset Caching (30 Days)
    location ~* \.(jpg|jpeg|png|gif|ico|css|js|svg|woff2)$ {
        expires 30d;
        add_header Cache-Control "public, no-transform";
    }
}


Save and exit (Ctrl + O $\rightarrow$ Enter $\rightarrow$ Ctrl + X).

Step 5: Enable Site & Reload Nginx
Link your new virtual host configuration, disable the default Nginx placeholder page, test syntax, and reload:

# 1. Enable new virtual host configuration
sudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/static-site /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/

*********
Why Nginx Uses Two Folders (sites-available vs sites-enabled)
Nginx uses this two-folder pattern to separate configuration storage from active deployment:

/etc/nginx/sites-available/ (The Storage Room):
Holds all configuration files you've written for various websites, whether they are live or offline. Nginx ignores files in this directory by default.

/etc/nginx/sites-enabled/ (The Live Stage):
Inside the main /etc/nginx/nginx.conf file, Nginx includes an import rule: include /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/*;. Anything inside this folder is active on the server.

⚙️ Practical Benefits
Zero File Duplication: Creating a symlink means you only edit the file once in sites-available. The link in sites-enabled automatically reflects any changes.

Instant Site Toggling:

To enable a site: Create the symlink (sudo ln -s ...).

To disable a site: Delete only the symlink (sudo rm /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/static-site). Your original file in sites-available remains completely safe.

*********

# 2. Disable Nginx default placeholder page
sudo rm -f /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/default

*********Deleting that file disables Nginx's built-in default placeholder website.
It Prevents Site Conflicts (Routing Overlaps)
By default, Ubuntu ships Nginx with a sample welcome page configured in /etc/nginx/sites-available/default and linked into /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/default.

That default config is set up to capture all incoming HTTP requests on Port 80 (listen 80 default_server;). If left active alongside your new site:

Nginx may get confused about which site to serve.

Visitors going to [http://50.17.122.32](http://50.17.122.32) might see the standard "Welcome to nginx!" page instead of your DevOps project.

*********

# 3. Test Nginx configuration syntax
sudo nginx -t

# 4. Reload Nginx service
sudo systemctl reload nginx

